   "title": "The dance of the honeybee",
   "passage": "**[1]** When a honeybee finds a rich patch of flowers, she does not keep the discovery to herself. Back in the dark hive, she performs a \"waggle dance\" on the surface of the comb while other workers crowd around and follow her movements with their antennae.\n\n**[2]** The dance has a simple pattern. The bee runs in a straight line while shaking her body from side to side, then circles back to the starting point and repeats the run. The direction of the straight run shows where the food is: a run pointing straight up means \"fly towards the sun\", while a run angled to the right means the flowers lie to the right of the sun. The length of the run shows distance. The longer the bee waggles, the farther away the food is.\n\n**[3]** The Austrian scientist Karl von Frisch worked out this code in the middle of the twentieth century, after years of marking individual bees with paint and watching them. Many researchers doubted him at first, but later experiments confirmed his findings, and he shared a Nobel Prize in 1973.\n\n**[4]** The dance is not used for every food source. For flowers very close to the hive, bees perform a simpler \"round dance\" that tells others only that food is nearby, without giving a direction.",
